#X text 413 607 Updated for version 0.57;
#X text 27 15 The previous example showed one way you can programmatically add and alter scalars. This one shows how to add a structure to the "put" menu so that you can easily add one to new windows. (If you've just made a new structure this is the easiest way to create its first scalar.), f 78;
#X text 26 84 It's also often convenient to specify initial values when new scalars are created. You can do this by adding the initial value for a field after its declaration \, as in the arguments "float z 20" \, etc \, in the [struct] for this example. Pd can tell that it's an initialization because it's a number and not a symbol. Either way \, if there's a following argument that one should be a symbol giving the next type (so far \, this has always been "float")., f 78;
#X msg 154 542 \; pd-data04 scalar struct04 175 200 80 50 600;
#X text 28 188 The struct04 window catches most editing actions and uses a [trigger] object to peel off the pointer in the message coming from [struct]. The messages have additional arguments that depend on the particular action. The patch prints them out so you can see them all., f 78;
#X text 29 255 It is sometimes useful to catch editing moves such as run-mode clicks and to display some or all of the field values so that you can see and/or modify them numerically. In this example it's done right in the "struct04" window., f 78;
#X text 81 458 You can also crweate scalars by sending a 'scalar' message to a window. The arguments of this message is template name and its field values in the order that the "struct" object lists them. This is intended as an internal message used to save and recall patches - use at your own risk., f 62;
#X text 31 308 The [struct] here doesn't include rectangles to show hot points (as the first example did) but you can still drag the top vertices of the triangles up and down (changing z) and the right ones left and right (changing w). These changes are reflected in the struct04 subpatch., f 78;
